---
import PermitNotice from '../components/PermitNotice.astro';
import SiteList from '../components/SiteList.astro';
import SiteMap from '../components/SiteMap.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { asset } from '../lib/paths';
import { loadSites, toMapSite } from '../lib/sites';

const all = await loadSites();
// North to south along the range.
const range = all
  .filter(({ site }) => site.data.mountainRange === 'Sierra Madre')
  .sort((a, b) => b.analysis.center[1] - a.analysis.center[1]);
const dinapigue = all.find(({ site }) => site.id === 'dinapigue');
const nsmnp = dinapigue?.analysis.protectedAreas.find((p) => p.id === 'nsmnp');
const km = (v: number) => `${v.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 1 })} km`;

const LAW = {
  ra9125: 'https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2001/ra_9125_2001.html',
  ra11038: 'https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2018/ra_11038_2018.html',
  enipasText: 'https://elibrary.judiciary.gov.ph/thebookshelf/showdocs/2/84999',
  ra7942: 'https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra1995/ra_7942_1995.html',
  proc413: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc2012/proc_413_2012.html',
};
const areas = [
  { name: 'Northern Sierra Madre Natural Park', where: 'Isabela', law: 'RA 9125 (2001)', url: LAW.ra9125 },
  { name: 'Peñablanca Protected Landscape and Seascape', where: 'Cagayan', law: 'Proc. 484 (2003); RA 11038', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc2003/proc_484_2003.html' },
  { name: 'Quirino Protected Landscape', where: 'Quirino', law: 'Proc. 548 (2004), Proc. 779 (2005)', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc2005/proc_779_2005.html' },
  { name: 'Casecnan Protected Landscape', where: 'Nueva Vizcaya, Quirino, Aurora', law: 'Proc. 289 (2000); RA 11038', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc2000/proc_289_2000.html' },
  { name: 'Aurora Memorial Park', where: 'Aurora, Nueva Ecija', law: 'Proc. 220 (1937), Proc. 130 (1949)', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc1949/proc_130_1949.html' },
  { name: 'Angat Watershed Reservation', where: 'Bulacan, Rizal, Nueva Ecija, Quezon', law: 'Proc. 71 (1927), Proc. 505 (1965)', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc1965/proc_505_1965.html' },
  { name: 'Pamitinan Protected Landscape', where: 'Rizal', law: 'Proc. 901 (1996); RA 11038', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc1996/proc_901_1996.html' },
  { name: 'Upper Marikina River Basin Protected Landscape', where: 'Rizal', law: 'Proc. 296 (2011); RA 11038', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc2011/proc_296_2011.html' },
  { name: 'Kaliwa River Forest Reserve', where: 'Quezon, Rizal', law: 'Proc. 573 (1969)', url: 'https://lawphil.net/executive/proc/proc1969/proc_573_1969.html' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Sierra Madre"
  description="Mines and quarries along the Sierra Madre in before-and-after satellite images, and what the law actually protects."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="label">Sierra Madre · satellite record</p>
      <h1>Mining along the Sierra Madre</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Sierra Madre runs down the Pacific side of Luzon, from Cagayan to Quezon. This page collects the
        mines and quarries on the range that are large enough to measure from space, the protected areas around
        them, and what the law protects.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="heads-up"><PermitNotice /></div>

    <SiteMap sites={range.map(toMapSite)} bounds={[120.6, 14.4, 122.8, 17.8]} height="560px" />
    <p class="small muted">
      Green outlines: protected areas whose boundaries are on this site so far. Click one for its law.
    </p>

    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Is the Sierra Madre protected by law?</h2>
      <p>
        Parts of it are. No single law covers the whole range.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={LAW.ra9125}>Republic Act 9125</a> (2001) created the Northern Sierra Madre Natural Park. It covers
        only the Isabela part of the range: 287,861 hectares of land and 71,625 hectares of coastal water. It
        bans <q>Exploration, exploitation or extraction, drilling or prospecting for minerals or resources, or
        engages in quarrying within the NSMNP or its buffer zones</q> (Section 21(h)), and sets those buffer
        zones at <q>not more than one (1) kilometer wide</q> from the park boundary (Section 5).
      </p>
      <p>
        Other parts of the range are protected one area at a time, each by its own proclamation or law. Inside
        the protected areas of the national system, the 2018 ENIPAS Act (<a href={LAW.ra11038}>RA 11038</a>)
        prohibits <q>Undertaking mineral exploration or extraction within the protected area</q> and
        <q>Engaging in commercial or large-scale quarrying within the protected area</q> (<a href={LAW.enipasText}
          >NIPAS Act Section 20, as amended</a
        >). Existing rights are respected, but their renewal is subject to the act. Separately, the Mining Act
        (<a href={LAW.ra7942}>RA 7942, Section 19</a>) closes proclaimed watershed forest reserves to new
        mineral agreements.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Protected area</th><th>Province</th><th>Declared by</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            areas.map((a) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{a.name}</td>
                <td>{a.where}</td>
                <td><a href={a.url}>{a.law}</a></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        September 26 is <a href={LAW.proc413}>Save Sierra Madre Day</a> (Proclamation 413, 2012). That is an
        observance: it does not set boundaries or ban mining. Bills to create an authority for the whole range
        have been filed in Congress; none had become law when this page was checked.
      </p>

      <h2>Dinapigue and the natural park</h2>
      <p>
        In May 2025 a photo of cleared forest in Dinapigue, Isabela went viral. The Federation of Environmental
        Advocates in Cagayan Valley called for a halt and an investigation into mining it said was inside the
        protected Sierra Madre. Dinapigue Mining Corporation, a Nickel Asia subsidiary, replied that <q>all our
        operations are conducted outside the boundaries of the Sierra Madre Natural Park</q> (<a
          href="https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/2063127/outcry-grows-over-alleged-mining-in-sierra-madre-protected-area"
          >Philippine Daily Inquirer, May 24, 2025</a
        >). At a 2025 stakeholders' forum, MGB Region II Director Mario Ancheta said the company had permits to
        mine and cut trees in a designated 15-hectare area <q>situated 1.1 kilometers from the protected zone</q>
        (<a
          href="https://region2.mgb.gov.ph/%F0%9D%97%97%F0%9D%97%B6%F0%9D%97%BB%F0%9D%97%AE%F0%9D%97%BD%F0%9D%97%B6%F0%9D%97%B4%F0%9D%98%82%F0%9D%97%B2-%F0%9D%97%A0%F0%9D%97%B6%F0%9D%97%BB%F0%9D%97%B2%F0%9D%98%80-%F0%9D%97%BF%F0%9D%97%B2/"
          >MGB Region II</a
        >).
      </p>
      {
        nsmnp && nsmnp.footprintDistanceKm !== null && (
          <p>
            This site draws the park boundary from the corner coordinates printed in RA 9125 itself, and checks it
            against the Biodiversity Management Bureau's own map of the park (they agree to within 36 metres). On
            that boundary, the mapped Dinapigue mine is <strong>{km(nsmnp.footprintDistanceKm)}</strong> from the
            park, beyond the 1 km buffer zone. None of the clearing around the mine pits reaches the park or its
            buffer; the few hectares the images count inside are along the shoreline. The{' '}
            <a href={asset('/sites/dinapigue/')}>Dinapigue page</a> has the images, with the park boundary as a
            layer. How the boundary is rebuilt, including two misprinted coordinates in the law, is on the{' '}
            <a href={asset('/methods/#protected-areas')}>Methods page</a>.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>What the images can't show here</h2>
      <p>
        The northern half of the range has few large open mines. Besides the Dinapigue nickel mine, MGB Region
        II's 2025 directory lists only sand-and-gravel permits in the range's towns of Peñablanca, Baggao and
        Gonzaga (Cagayan), San Mariano (Isabela) and Maddela (Quirino), from under 1 hectare
        to about 20 hectares each (<a
          href="https://region2.mgb.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Directory-of-Operating-Mines-and-Quarries.pdf"
          >MGB Region II</a
        >). Most of those are in riverbeds and are too small to measure reliably at 10–30 m. Unpermitted
        small-scale digging is not in any of these records.
      </p>
      <p>
        The biggest bare ground on the range is at its southern end, in the limestone and rock quarries of
        Bulacan and Rizal that supply cement and aggregates to Metro Manila. The published mine-outline dataset
        used elsewhere on this site does not map them, so their boxes come from MGB permit lists, EMB
        environmental documents and the 2026 Sentinel-2 images.
      </p>
    </section>

    <SiteList title="Sites on the Sierra Madre, north to south" sites={range} />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding: 2.4rem 0 1.6rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .head .label {
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .heads-up {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .small {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .prose {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.5rem 0.35rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  th {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
</style>
